          {
            "url": "https://www.politico.com/story/2019/02/27/china-trade-deal-lighthizer-1222220",
            "title": "Lighthizer plays down a quick deal on China - POLITICO",
            "snippets": [
              "# Lighthizer plays down a quick deal on China\nU.S. Trade Representative Robert Lighthizer told Congress on Wednesday it was unclear whether the United States and China could resolve a high-stakes trade war, contradicting the claims of his boss, President Donald Trump, who recently trumpeted that the two sides are “very, very close.”\n\n“Let me be clear: much still needs to be done both before an agreement is reached and, more importantly, after it is reached, if one is reached,” Lighthizer told the House Ways and Means Committee during a hearing on the administration’s trade talks with Beijing",
              "“There is no trade program in the United States if we don’t pass USMCA,” Lighthizer told lawmakers of a key panel that will vote on the pact. “It just has to pass. If it doesn’t, you have no credibility at all with China and you will have no credibility on any deals with your other trading partners.”\n\nLighthizer said that although he expects any agreement struck with China to be strong and enforceable, he recognizes that any initial agreement will require rigorous follow-up, and new problems will likely emerge",
              "“I’m not foolish enough to think that there’s going to be one negotiation that’s going to change all the practices of China or our relationship with them,” Lighthizer said.\n\nHe added later: “Agreements between nations, like flowers and children, last while they last.”\n\nThroughout the more than three-hour hearing — the first Democrats have held on trade since taking control of the chamber — Lighthizer told lawmakers that negotiations with Beijing are “making real progress” and “could help us turn the corner in our economic relationship with China.”",
              "But that depends on China agreeing to change policies and practices that harm American companies and accepting strong enforcement provisions that will allow the United States to punish the country if it fails to honor the promises it makes, Lighthizer said.\n\n“I don’t think we should accept anything that doesn’t have structural changes and is enforceable. Absolutely not,” he said",
              "The comments from Lighthizer, who has for decades railed against China’s trade practices, were aimed at soothing concerns among lawmakers and industry officials who worry that Trump might accept a smaller-scale deal focused primarily on commodity purchases.\n\nTrump announced earlier this week he would delay his plan to raise a 10 percent duty on the $200 billion worth of Chinese goods to 25 percent to give the two sides more time to strike a deal. Lighthizer’s office said Wednesday that the tariff increase would be suspended “until further notice.”",
              "The U.S. already has in place a 25 percent duty on another $50 billion worth of Chinese goods and Beijing has retaliated by hitting $110 billion of American exports. During the questioning, Lighthizer declined to say whether an agreement would lead to Trump removing all the duties on Beijing, but noted is “certainly the objective of the Chinese that the tariffs go away.”\n\nEarlier on Wednesday, Senate Minority Leader Chuck Schumer said on the Senate floor that Trump “seems headed down the path of capitulation“ with China",
              "“His attention span is so small, his desire for immediate gratification seems to be so large, that the American worker loses,” Schumer said. “And if we capitulate to China, that American worker loses for decades. That American worker’s children will lose.”\n\nLighthizer sought at the hearing to tamp down such concerns by insisting that the U.S. would reserve its right to take unilateral action to enforce commitments — signaling that the deal would not be subject to a dispute-settlement process or the dispute process at the World Trade Organization",
              "Although Trump has said he looked forward to signing a deal with Chinese President Xi Jinping as early as March, Lighthizer gave no indication when such a summit would take place. He also did not reveal a new deadline for reaching a deal, after Trump decided earlier this week to extend talks beyond the previous March 1 deadline.\n\nFor the first time, Lighthizer outlined on Wednesday the process under which China’s commitments would be upheld",
              "“Hopefully, in most cases, they will be resolved at the first or second level. If not, they will be resolved at my level. And if there is disagreement at my level, then the United States would be expected to act proportionally but unilaterally to insist on enforcement,” Lighthizer said.\n\nBut critics said the provisions sounded very similar to past administration’s efforts to address problems through regular dialogue, such as the U.S.-China Joint Commission on Commerce and Trade, which Lighthizer himself has skewered",
              "“It’s just not clear what is materially different,” said one source regularly briefed on the talks. The source also questioned how unilateral enforcement would be different from the existing authority Trump used to impose the tariffs and whether the “proportional“ retaliation described by Lighthizer would actually serve as a deterrent.\n\n“To actually move the needle you have to bring an overwhelming amount of leverage,” the source said. “It’s a huge economy with a high tolerance for pain.”",
              "Lighthizer also indicated to lawmakers that there isn’t a formal deal to address China’s currency manipulation despite Trump’s comments last week that world’s two largest economies had reached one.\n\n“There’s no agreement on anything until there’s an agreement on everything. But the reality is we have spent a lot of time on currency and it’ll be enforceable,” he said",
              "He added that a deal must include assurances that the country won’t devalue its currency in the future for competitive reasons and said Beijing has made commitments that match that. But a currency agreement is only seen as one piece of what is expected to be a much larger deal between the two countries.\n\nMeanwhile, Ways and Means Chairman Richard Neal (D-Mass.) said he was optimistic that the Trump administration is working toward a final deal that will have strong enforcement mechanisms, citing Lighthizer’s “credibility” on the issue",
              "The U.S. trade chief has “been vigorous in his decision about enforcement,” Neal told reporters after the hearing, adding that he believes the bilateral negotiations will lead to more than just talk from Chinese officials.\n\nLighthizer, however, added that the administration will “in the next day or so” be taking steps to formalize Trump’s announcement over the weekend that he will not be hiking tariffs on $200 billion in Chinese goods from 10 percent to 25 percent, as he had previously threatened",
              "He said Trump deserves credit for getting Chinese attention through his aggressive use of duties. “This is the first time I believe it’s been approached in this way and it’s really the result of the creation of an enormous amount of leverage by the president,” Lighthizer said"
            ]
          },
          {
            "url": "https://www.nytimes.com/2019/02/26/us/politics/robert-lighthizer-china-trade.html",
            "title": "Trump Undermines Top Trade Adviser as He Pushes for China Deal ...",
            "snippets": [
              "# Trump Undermines Top Trade Adviser as He Pushes for China Deal\nRobert Lighthizer, the United States trade representative, has been in monthslong negotiations with China’s vice premier, Liu He, as he seeks a tough trade deal with Beijing.",
              "The situation has left Mr. Trump’s trade representative, Robert Lighthizer, who is both an ardent supporter of the president and a longtime China critic, in an uncomfortable bind. While broad tariffs on Chinese imports brought Beijing to the negotiating table, Mr. Trump has grown impatient with the talks, and a consensus is growing in Washington that Mr. Trump will ultimately accept a weak deal",
              "And despite the lack of a transformative arrangement he once promised, the president has begun dangling the idea of a “signing summit” with President Xi Jinping of China at Mar-a-Lago, Mr. Trump’s Florida resort. As a result, the president is undermining Mr. Lighthizer as he tries to pressure China to make big concessions",
              "“Trump is certainly doing his negotiating team no favors by undercutting them in public,” said Eswar Prasad, a trade expert and the former head of the China division of the International Monetary Fund. The president’s actions, he said, “weakens rather than fortifies Lighthizer’s leverage.”\n\nOn Wednesday, Mr. Lighthizer will testify before House lawmakers, where he will have to credibly defend a pact that is shaping up to be less ambitious than he might have hoped",
              "Mr. Trump continues to insist that any deal with China will end “unfair” trade practices, including what he has said is theft of American technology and intellectual property. And negotiators are trying to insert an enforcement mechanism into any deal that would require China to return to the bargaining table if it fails to make these structural changes, a person familiar with the talks said",
              "But the hardened position that prompted Mr. Trump’s unprecedented imposition of sweeping tariffs on Chinese goods appears to be weakening, giving Beijing more room to maneuver and undermining Mr. Lighthizer’s goal",
              "On Sunday, citing “substantial progress” in trade talks, Mr. Trump backed off his March 1 deadline to impose higher tariffs on $200 billion worth of Chinese goods. That date had been described by Mr. Lighthizer in December as a “hard deadline” and was viewed as critical to getting Beijing to agree to significant changes since its economy, already hurting from tariffs, would suffer more from higher levies",
              "Last week, Mr. Trump contradicted Mr. Lighthizer by lumping the fate of Huawei, the Chinese telecom giant facing criminal charges, in with the trade talks. Mr. Lighthizer had insisted that Huawei was “entirely a criminal justice matter” and that it had “nothing to do with anything I’m working on.” But on Friday, when asked whether criminal charges against Huawei could be dropped as part of an agreement, Mr. Trump said: “We’ll be talking to the U.S. attorneys",
              "“His intervention has now ratcheted up the pressure on both sets of negotiators to find a path to an agreement in the coming weeks, even if the result should be a narrow and short-lived agreement,” Mr. Prasad said.\n\nThe differences between the two men mounted on Friday, as they shared a remarkable exchange in front of the press and the Chinese delegation over whether to call the current trade agreement a memorandum of understanding, or M.O.U., as Mr. Lighthizer had been doing",
              "Mr. Trump, seemingly unhappy with being contradicted, cut back in.\n\nVideo\n\nTrump and Trade Adviser ‘Disagree’ on What to Call Deal With China\n\nIn the Oval Office, President Trump quibbled with his top trade adviser, Robert Lighthizer, over the nature of a memorandum of understanding, or M.O.U. From now on, Mr. Lighthizer said, he won’t use that term.Credit Credit...Carlos Barria/Reuters",

⚠️ [... middle content omitted — showing head and tail ...]

